# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PETSc solver options for JOREK.
#
# Copy this file to  jorek.petsc  in the run directory and uncomment what you
# need. It is read automatically at startup when present; if it is absent JOREK
# runs with its built-in defaults, which are exactly the settings listed as
# "default:" below.
#
# Format is the standard PETSc options file: one option per line, '#' comments.
# Anything set here overrides JOREK's compiled-in default.
#
# Three prefixes exist:
#   -jorek_          the main iterative (GMRES) solve
#   -jorek_pcblock_  the direct solver inside every preconditioner block
#   -jorek_direct_   the one-shot direct solve path
#
# The -jorek_pcblock_ prefix is shared by all toroidal mode-family blocks, so a
# single option below reconfigures every one of them.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# --- Operator storage format ------------------------------------------------
# How the system matrix is built and handed to the KSP.
#
#   baij (default)  MATMPIBAIJ, filled block-by-block with MatSetValuesBlocked
#                   straight from the element loop, then MatConvert'ed to
#                   MATMPIAIJ for the KSP on EVERY solve.
#   aij             JOREK assembles into its own val array and pushes it to a
#                   MATAIJ operator with one MatSetValuesCOO; the COO indices are
#                   derived from the block sparsity, so irn/jcn are not even
#                   allocated. The KSP consumes that matrix directly - no
#                   conversion, and no b/x vector copies.
#
# Use aij for anything GPU: PETSc implements the COO interface and the device
# matrix types (aijcusparse, aijkokkos) for AIJ only, never for BAIJ, so baij
# can never be device-resident. On CPU the two agree to solver tolerance -
# identical GMRES iteration counts - and differ only in assembly cost.
# -jorek_mat_format          aij        # default: baij
#
# With aij you can additionally pick the concrete PETSc matrix type, e.g.
# -jorek_mat_type            aijcusparse
# The solution/RHS vectors are created with MatCreateVecs and so follow the
# matrix type automatically - there is no separate vector option to set.
#
# -jorek_mat_type is read ONLY on the aij path, because that is the only path
# that calls MatSetFromOptions on the operator. Setting it without also setting
# -jorek_mat_format aij leaves it unused, which -options_left reports as
#   Option left: name:-jorek_mat_type value:aijcusparse
# The log line says which path ran: "[PETSc] init: AIJ/COO matrix ..." for aij,
# "[PETSc] create_matrix: BAIJ ..." for baij.
#
# MEMORY: aij costs about 16 bytes per nonzero more than baij at steady state -
# JOREK's val array (8) plus PETSc's persistent COO map (16), against the
# converted-AIJ duplicate baij carries (8). At ~3e8 nonzeros per rank that is
# ~2.4 GB, which is enough to turn a job that just fits into one that is killed.
# Raise the memory request before switching a large case over.
#
# NOTE on use_matrix_equilibration: equilibration indexes through jcn and the
# block-CSR map on every solve, so with it enabled the aij path keeps both and
# the memory saving above does not apply. Be aware also that equilibration is
# currently applied only when PETSc is NOT assembling the matrix directly, so
# baij + use_matrix_equilibration silently skips it while aij does not - the two
# formats are then not comparable.
#
# -jorek_coo_verify_indices             # debugging: cross-check the generated
#     COO indices against the irn/jcn the element loop fills, and abort on any
#     disagreement. Keeps irn/jcn alive for the comparison, so it costs the
#     memory the aij path normally saves. Use after touching either the block
#     structure or the index convention in add_block_to_sp_matrix.

# --- Main iterative solve ---------------------------------------------------
# -jorek_ksp_type            gmres      # default: gmres
# -jorek_ksp_rtol            1e-8       # default: 1e-8
# -jorek_ksp_max_it          400        # default: 400
# -jorek_ksp_gmres_restart   40         # default: 40
# -jorek_ksp_view                       # dump the full solver configuration
#
# Note: -jorek_pc_type is not a supported knob. The outer preconditioner carries
# the toroidal mode-family decomposition and is structural. What you can choose
# is how that decomposition is laid out across the ranks - see the next section.

# --- Parallel layout of the mode-family preconditioner ----------------------
# The preconditioner is block-diagonal in the toroidal mode families. There are
# two ways to spread those blocks over the ranks, and they differ enormously in
# parallel efficiency.
#
#   PCFIELDSPLIT (default)  every block lives on the full communicator, and the
#                           blocks are applied one after another. Each block is
#                           therefore factorized across ALL ranks - exactly the
#                           regime in which a sparse direct solver stops scaling.
#
#   -jorek_pc_mode_split    each mode family gets its own disjoint MPI
#                           sub-communicator, holding that family's sub-matrix
#                           and its own direct solver. Because the communicators
#                           are disjoint, all blocks are factorized and solved
#                           CONCURRENTLY, each by a smaller set of ranks. This is
#                           the layout JOREK's legacy (non-PETSc) solver has
#                           always used.
#
# -jorek_pc_mode_split                  # default: off (PCFIELDSPLIT)
#
# Which mode families exist, and how many ranks each gets, come from the JOREK
# namelist, not from here - the same variables the legacy path uses:
#   autodistribute_modes, n_mode_families, modes_per_family, mode_families_modes,
#   weights_per_family, autodistribute_ranks, ranks_per_family
# With autodistribute_modes (the default) there is one family per toroidal
# harmonic - family 1 is the n=0 mode alone, every other family is a sin/cos
# pair - and autodistribute_ranks spreads the ranks over them evenly.
#
# REQUIREMENT: at least one rank per mode family, so n_ranks >= n_mode_families.
# JOREK says so and stops if that is not met. Family 1 holds half as many modes
# as the others, so its block is smaller; ranks_per_family is how you compensate,
# e.g. ranks_per_family = 1,2,2,2 with autodistribute_ranks = .f.
#
# Startup prints the resulting partition:
#   [PETSc] mode-split PC: 2 mode families over 2 ranks
#     family 1: 1 rank(s), weight 1.00, modes: 1
#     family 2: 1 rank(s), weight 1.00, modes: 2 3
#
# The blocks are configured exactly as under PCFIELDSPLIT: everything below under
# the -jorek_pcblock_ prefix applies unchanged.

# Under -jorek_pc_mode_split each rebuild also reports where its time went:
#   [PETSc] mode-split PCSetUp: extraction   1.2345E-01 s, factorization   4.5678E+00 s
# Extraction is carving the family operators out of the global one - a collective
# on the full communicator, paid by every rank. Factorization is the family-local
# direct solve setup, and the only half that concurrency or any future background
# scheme can act on. The factorization figure is family 1's, measured on rank 0;
# the families are not synchronized against each other, so it is a lower bound on
# the slowest one.

# --- REQUIRED when MUMPS factorizes across more than one rank ---------------
# MUMPS' distributed-RHS path (ICNTL(20)=10, PETSc's default for parallel MUMPS)
# reads out of bounds inside dmumps_scatter_dist_rhs on at least some builds. The
# damage is silent: it usually does not crash, it corrupts the heap, and the run
# goes on to produce WRONG ANSWERS. Forcing a centralized RHS avoids that path.
#
# Set it for EVERY prefix that runs MUMPS on more than one rank. Missing one is
# enough to reintroduce the bug - the equilibrium solve is easy to forget:
#
# -jorek_pcblock_mat_mumps_icntl_20 0   # PC block solves (fieldsplit and mode-split)
# -jorek_direct_mat_mumps_icntl_20 0    # the direct/equilibrium solve
#
# How to tell whether you need it: run any two of the preconditioners on the same
# case and compare solution norms. They must agree. If they do not, you are seeing
# this bug. A stronger check is macOS Guard Malloc:
#   mpirun -n 2 -x DYLD_INSERT_LIBRARIES=/usr/lib/libgmalloc.dylib ./jorek_model... 
# which turns the silent corruption into an immediate, located crash.
#
# Not needed when every factorization lands on exactly one rank (e.g.
# -jorek_pc_mode_split with n_ranks == n_mode_families and a serial equilibrium).

# --- Direct solver used in each preconditioner block ------------------------
# Which factorization package. Requires the package in your PETSc build;
# PETSc reports clearly at startup if it is missing.
# -jorek_pcblock_pc_factor_mat_solver_type mumps          # default
# -jorek_pcblock_pc_factor_mat_solver_type superlu_dist
# -jorek_pcblock_pc_factor_mat_solver_type strumpack
#
# Note: PETSc's built-in LU ('petsc') is NOT usable here - the blocks are MPIAIJ
# and it only factorizes SEQAIJ. PETSc says so clearly if you try. The same
# applies to cuDSS, which is why it needs the telescope recipe further down
# rather than appearing in the list above.
#
# MUMPS controls. JOREK's defaults are ICNTL(7)=7 (METIS ordering),
# ICNTL(14)=50 (workspace %), ICNTL(8)=77 (auto scaling), ICNTL(22)=0 (in-core).
# Setting one here overrides just that one; the rest keep JOREK's values.
# -jorek_pcblock_mat_mumps_icntl_22 1    # 1 = out-of-core factorization
# -jorek_pcblock_mat_mumps_icntl_7  5
#
# SuperLU_DIST / STRUMPACK / cuDSS controls follow the same pattern:
# -jorek_pcblock_mat_superlu_dist_colperm PARMETIS
# -jorek_pcblock_mat_strumpack_compression NONE
#
# The block solver itself can also be replaced wholesale, e.g. to try an
# iterative block solve instead of a direct one:
# -jorek_pcblock_ksp_type richardson
# -jorek_pcblock_pc_type  hypre

# --- Sequential block solvers, via PCTELESCOPE ------------------------------
# A sequential package (cuDSS, or PETSc's built-in LU) cannot factorize an
# MPIAIJ block directly. PCTELESCOPE reduces the block onto a smaller
# communicator first; with a reduction factor equal to the number of ranks that
# communicator has size 1, and PETSc then resolves the block to SEQAIJ, which
# those packages do accept.
#
# -jorek_pcblock_pc_type                        telescope
# -jorek_pcblock_pc_telescope_reduction_factor  4          # = number of MPI ranks
# -jorek_pcblock_telescope_pc_factor_mat_solver_type cudss
#
# Everything the block solver understands moves under the extra 'telescope_'
# prefix, JOREK's own defaults included: selecting telescope and nothing else
# still gives LU via MUMPS with JOREK's ICNTLs, and an override is written
# -jorek_pcblock_telescope_mat_mumps_icntl_14 20
#
# Caveat when comparing against MUMPS: MUMPS factorizes the block across all
# ranks, whereas a telescoped block is factorized on one. Telescope both, or
# compare on a single rank, or the numbers are not measuring the same thing.
#
# ON MORE THAN ONE RANK, PREFER -jorek_pc_mode_split TO THIS RECIPE. PETSc's
# PetscSubcomm_isActiveRank puts colour 0 on rank 0 for every reduction factor
# and subcomm type, so every telescoped block is reduced onto the SAME rank: one
# GPU does all the work and the rest idle, and no prefix or factor changes that.
# The mode-split PC gives each family its own communicator instead, so at one
# rank per family PETSc resolves each block to SEQAIJ - what cuDSS needs - on a
# DIFFERENT rank, and every GPU is busy:
#
#   -jorek_pc_mode_split
#   -jorek_pcblock_pc_factor_mat_solver_type cudss
#
# Telescope remains the right tool for reducing a block below one rank per
# family, or for reaching cuDSS reorderings only available on a single rank.

# --- One-shot direct solve path ---------------------------------------------
# -jorek_direct_pc_factor_mat_solver_type mumps           # default

# --- Matrix dumping ---------------------------------------------------------
# Write the operators JOREK actually solves with to PETSc binary files, so they
# can be replayed offline against different factorization packages (e.g. with
# PETSc's src/mat/tests/ex125 -f <file>). Off unless set; costs nothing when absent.
# -jorek_dump_mat  jorekmat        # -> jorekmat_system.dat, jorekmat_pcblock1.dat, ...

# --- Diagnostics ------------------------------------------------------------
# -options_left                         # report unused (i.e. misspelled) options
# -log_view                             # PETSc performance summary
